<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The launcher icon (Task 66; the CoralX mark since 2026-09-12).

  Three layers, not a bitmap for the whole icon: the launcher masks background and
  foreground to its own shape and parallaxes them, and <monochrome> is what lets Android
  13+ tint the icon to the user's wallpaper instead of dropping a coloured square into a
  themed home screen.

  ## The foreground is a bitmap now, and that is a change of premise rather than of mind

  It used to be a vector, on the argument that one file beats five density buckets nobody
  can review in a diff. That argument holds while the glyph is ours to draw. The CoralX
  mark is not: it is supplied artwork, and its coral-to-magenta and cyan-to-teal ramps,
  the soft inner shading of the C and the glow around both strokes are not a path list.
  Tracing it would ship something that is nearly the brand, which is worse than shipping
  the brand.

  So the mark ships as PNGs at all five densities, generated from the supplied file and
  keyed off its own background - the near-black disc inside the C is kept, because it is
  part of the mark and not part of the ground. The plate behind it stays a vector, and its
  colour is the median of the pixels the mark was keyed off, so the two cannot drift apart.

  Every layer is 108dp with the mark inside the 66% the launcher is guaranteed to keep,
  which is what stops a circular mask from cutting the ends off the X.
-->
<adaptive-icon xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <background android:drawable="@drawable/ic_launcher_background" />
    <foreground android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_foreground" />
    <monochrome android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_monochrome" />
</adaptive-icon>
